    "details": "Mongoose is an embedded web server and network library. Prior to 7.23, a network attacker can impersonate a TLS server to a Mongoose client configured with a multi-certificate CA bundle. In `src/tls_builtin.c`, the `mg_tls_init()` function stores the bundle in tls->`ca_bundle_der` while tls->`ca_der.len` remains zero, and `mg_tls_recv_cert()` uses `tls_bundle_find()` to accept a Common Name match without calling `mg_tls_verify_cert_signature()`. A forged self-signed certificate can therefore satisfy hostname and CertificateVerify checks and enable interception, credential disclosure, traffic modification, and malicious responses. This issue is fixed in version 7.23.",
